#Chapter Nine: Out of the Fairytale
The phone in Shiv’s coat pocket buzzed with a sharp, synchronized vibration just as he finished loading the last canvas duffel into the trunk of the heavy-duty off-road SUV.

Stepping a few paces away from the vehicle, Shiv pulled the phone to his ear, his voice dropping into a low, guarded murmur. "Report."

"Perimeter's clean, Sir," KK’s voice came through the receiver, hushed and tight with lingering urgency. "The trail from the ridge to the cabin is clear of heat signatures. We swept the interior twice. No signs of a prior entry, but the locks on the back storm cellar were stiff. Someone tried to test them within the last forty-eight hours."

Shiv’s jaw tightened, his eyes darkening. "Keep the comms secure. We're moving out now."

He hung up, slipping the phone back into his pocket, and turned around to find Ira standing just a few feet away near the edge of the stone driveway. She had frozen mid-step, her dark eyes wide and searching as she looked at him. Even though his voice had been a whisper, the raw, unyielding tension in his posture hadn't escaped her.

"Everything alright, Shiv?" Ira asked quietly, her heart giving an uneasy flutter against her ribs.

Shiv’s expression instantly softened, smoothing away the hard edges of the threat with practiced ease. He offered a reassuring, effortless smile as he walked closer to her. "Just a minor logistical hiccup with the resort staff about weekend supplies. Nothing for you to worry about."

Ira wanted to believe him. Every instinct told her that his forced casualness was a mask—that a hushed phone call about "supplies" didn't require that level of grim intensity. Yet, looking at the way the cool mountain wind caught her hair, she pushed the doubt aside, deciding to trust the man who had pulled her out of the fire.

Besides, beneath the lingering shadow of unease, a strange, bubbling warmth fluttered in her chest. A real family trip. A chance to step outside the suffocating walls of the mansion and just... exist.

Across the driveway, Mrs. Kashyap watched them from the passenger window of the SUV, a soft, genuine smile touching her lips as she caught the quiet exchange between her son and their guest. For the first time in months, she saw the heavy, exhausting burden lift from Shiv's shoulders, even if just a fraction. He was actually smiling.

"Alright, everyone pile in," Shiv announced, opening the driver's door and settling behind the wheel. "Hold on to your seats. This road isn't for the faint-hearted."

The journey up into the upper woods was an absolute test of gravity and horsepower. Shiv navigated the rugged, unpaved off-road tracks with expert precision, the heavy tires crunching over loose gravel and thick pine needles as they climbed higher and higher into the dense, misty canopy of Manali's wilderness.

Inside the cabin, Sindhura groaned good-naturedly as the SUV bounced over a root. "Bhai, are you trying to shake our teeth loose before we even get to the marshmallows?"

"Builds character, Sindhura," Shiv called back over his shoulder, a rare, amused glint in his dark eyes.

After a grueling forty-minute ascent through winding mountain trails, the vehicle broke through a thick curtain of towering cedar trees and came to a halt in a secluded clearing.

Nestled deep in the heart of the woods stood the hunting cabin. It was a masterpiece of rustic charm—built from dark, weathered timber and river stone, featuring a cozy wrap-around porch, a massive stone chimney, and a welcoming warmth radiating from the windows. Inside, the main floor housed an open-plan living hall seamlessly connected to a country kitchen and dining area, flanked by only two bedrooms.

"Oh, this is gorgeous," Mandhira sighed happily, stepping out of the car and breathing in the crisp, pine-scented air.

But it wasn't the main cabin that caught Ira's attention. Just a few yards away, standing like something straight out of a childhood storybook, was a magnificent wooden treehouse built into the sprawling branches of a massive ancient oak tree. A sturdy wooden staircase wound up its trunk, leading to a charming wrap-around balcony and two cozy rooms nestled among the leaves.

For Ira, it was love at first sight. Her eyes lit up with an unfiltered, childlike wonder. "Is that... a treehouse?" she whispered aloud, completely transfixed by the panoramic view of the misty valley visible from the upper branches.

Shiv caught the look on her face as he stepped out of the driver's seat. A flicker of hesitation crossed his features—keeping her close in the main cabin where security was tight made logical sense. But seeing the pure, unadulterated joy lighting up her eyes, his protective instincts melted into something dangerously tender.

"It's the old lookout loft," Shiv said, walking over to join her as she stared up at the branches. "Used to spend all my summers up there as a kid."

"It's breathtaking," Ira murmured, looking up at him with a soft, genuine smile. "I didn't know places like this actually existed outside of fairy tales."

Before Shiv could voice his internal debate about security, Mrs. Kashyap walked up behind them, glancing between her son and the treehouse with a knowing look. "Well, what are we standing around for? The main cabin has the kitchen, but those rooms up in the treehouse have the best view in the entire forest. Shiv, take one of them."

"Ma, security-wise—" Shiv started to object, though a subtle smile tugged at the corner of his mouth.

"Nonsense," Mrs. Kashyap interrupted gently, patting his arm. "We never say no to a guest enjoying the property. Ira can take the other room up in the treehouse."

Mandhira grinned mischievously. "Ooh, look at that! Bhai gets promoted from sullen office worker to woodland treehouse host."

Shiv looked down at Ira, raising an eyebrow in silent question, a playful challenge dancing in his dark eyes. "Well. The master has spoken. Do you trust me enough to bunk above the ground?"

Ira laughed, a bright, melodic sound that chased away the last lingering shadows of the city. "Only if you promise there are no wild bears."

"No bears," Shiv promised softly, his gaze lingering on her just a second too long. "Just us."

As KK stepped out of the secondary security vehicle and calmly surveyed the perimeter, he offered a dry nod. "I'll take the living room couch by the fireplace. Someone has to make sure the wildlife doesn't raid the pantry."

With the rooms claimed and the forest settling into a quiet, golden dusk, the group unpacked their bags, completely unaware of the violent storm gathering just beyond the tree line, ready to test the fragile peace of their woodland sanctuary.
